1. Pod is in Pending State ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer:

"A Pod is stuck in Pending state. What will you do?"

Possible Causes
Insufficient CPU/Memory
No available nodes
PVC not bound
Taints and tolerations mismatch
Node selector/affinity mismatch

Commands
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>
Sample Answer

First, I check the pod status using kubectl get pods. Then I run kubectl describe pod to see the Events section. Usually, it tells me if the pod couldn't be scheduled due to insufficient resources, node affinity, or storage issues. Based on the reason, I either free resources, scale the cluster, or fix the scheduling configuration.

2. Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer:

"Your Pod is restarting continuously. How do you troubleshoot?"

Causes
Application crash
Wrong environment variables
Database connection failure
Missing Secret
Wrong startup command

Commands
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

Sample Answer
I first check the container logs to identify why the application is crashing. Then I verify environment variables, ConfigMaps, Secrets, and database connectivity. If necessary, I inspect the deployment YAML for incorrect commands or image configurations.

3. ImagePullBackOff ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer:

"Your image is not downloading."

Causes
Wrong image tag
Wrong image name
Private registry authentication failure
Image doesn't exist

Check
kubectl describe pod

Look for
Failed to pull image


4. Node is NotReady ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer:

"One worker node becomes NotReady. What happens?"

Answer
Scheduler stops scheduling new Pods on that node.
Existing Pods may become unavailable.
Kubernetes recreates Pods on healthy nodes (depending on the controller and timing).
Investigate the node's kubelet, network, disk, or cloud instance health.

Commands
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node

5. Service is Not Accessible ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer
"Pods are running but the application is not accessible."

Check
kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc

Verify
Selector labels
TargetPort
Port
Endpoints

Example
Pod

labels:
 app: backend

Service

selector:
 app: frontend

No matching Pods.

6. Deployment Failed After New Release ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer

"Deployment failed after releasing a new version."

Answer

Check rollout
kubectl rollout status deployment myapp

Rollback
kubectl rollout undo deployment myapp

Mention that rolling updates minimize downtime and allow quick rollback if the new version is unhealthy.

7. ConfigMap/Secret Issue ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer

"Application is not starting after configuration changes."

Possible issue
Secret Missing
ConfigMap Missing
Error
CreateContainerConfigError

Check
kubectl describe pod

8. OOMKilled ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer
"Container gets killed automatically."

Reason
Memory exceeded.
limits:
 memory: 512Mi

Application uses
900Mi
Status
OOMKilled

Fix

Increase memory limits.
Optimize the application.
Set appropriate resource requests and limits.


9. Readiness Probe Failure ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer
"Application is running but users can't access it."

Reason
Readiness Probe Failed
Container is running.
Application isn't ready.
Traffic won't reach that Pod until the readiness probe succeeds.

10. Liveness Probe Failure ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer
"Container keeps restarting automatically."

Reason
Liveness probe fails.
Kubernetes assumes the application is unhealthy and restarts the container.

11. Persistent Volume Issue ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer
"Database Pod is Pending."

Reason
PVC not bound.

Commands
kubectl get pvc
kubectl describe pvc

12. DNS Resolution Failure ⭐⭐⭐
Interviewer
"Frontend cannot communicate with Backend."

Check
nslookup backend-service
kubectl exec -it pod -- sh

Also verify:
Service name
Namespace
DNS configuration

13. High CPU Usage ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Interviewer
"Pods are slow."

Check
kubectl top pod
kubectl top node

Solution
Scale Pods (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler).
Increase CPU requests/limits.
Optimize the application.


14. Worker Node Disk Full ⭐⭐⭐

Symptoms
Pods Evicted
Node Not Ready

Check
df -h

Clean
Old container images
Logs
Unused volumes


15. Cluster Upgrade Failure ⭐⭐⭐

Interviewer
"Upgrade failed."

Answer
Upgrade control plane first.
Upgrade worker nodes.
Drain nodes before upgrading.
Uncordon nodes after completion.
Verify workloads after each step.

Commands
kubectl drain node1
kubectl uncordon node1
Real Interview Scenario (Most Common)


Interviewer:

"Your application is down. Walk me through your troubleshooting process."

A strong answer would be:

1. kubectl get pods
        │
        ▼
2. Check Pod Status
        │
        ▼
3. kubectl describe pod
        │
        ▼
4. kubectl logs
        │
        ▼
5. kubectl get events
        │
        ▼
6. kubectl get svc
        │
        ▼
7. kubectl get ingress
        │
        ▼
8. kubectl get nodes
        │
        ▼
9. Check CPU/Memory
        │
        ▼
10. Rollback if required



Interview Tip

Interviewers usually don't expect you to memorize every error message. They want to see a logical troubleshooting process:

Identify the failing component (Pod, Service, Node, Ingress, Storage).
Gather evidence using kubectl get, describe, logs, and events.
Find the root cause (application, configuration, networking, storage, or resources).
Resolve the issue and verify that the application is healthy again
